<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!--
     MT6750 power profile adapted to this handset.

     The owner supplied a Vivo-tuned MT6750 profile. The installed cell is
     printed 3.8 V / 12.16 Wh = 3200 mAh nominal (teardown, E-176; this
     supersedes E-104's 4000 mAh label reading). The CPU
     topology and OPPs below are this handset's measured values, not the
     donor's. Android 10 no longer reads the donor's single cpu.speeds /
     cpu.active arrays, so its active-current curve is linearly interpolated by
     frequency onto both measured clusters. Component currents are copied only
     where Android 10 has the same accounting key. Vivo's OLED/color and other
     proprietary keys are deliberately omitted: this handset has an FT8057S
     LCD and AOSP does not consume them.

     This file is BatteryStats accounting metadata. It does not select CPU
     frequencies or change the screen-on PPM/PowerHAL policy.
-->
<device name="Android">
    <item name="ambient.on">0</item>
    <item name="screen.on">60</item>
    <item name="screen.full">295</item>
    <!-- KNOWN GAP, recorded so the next reader does not have to rediscover it:
         Bluetooth is never attributed to any app in the Settings battery
         breakdown on this build, and these two keys are not what would fix it.
         On Q their only consumer is PowerProfile.writePowerConstantToProto
         (PowerProfile.java:641,644), i.e. a dumpsys proto field and nothing
         else. The keys that drive blame are bluetooth.controller.idle/rx/tx,
         read by BluetoothPowerCalculator.java:31-33; absent, they read 0, and
         calculateApp()'s powerMah==0 fallback at :52-55 then computes zero for
         every app. Unlike Wi-Fi, there is no estimator fallback:
         BatteryStatsHelper.java:416-419 swaps WifiPowerCalculator for
         WifiPowerEstimator when the chipset does not report energy, which is
         why wifi.on/active/scan below are live, but :426-429 constructs
         BluetoothPowerCalculator unconditionally.

         The three keys are NOT added here because there is no measured value
         for this handset and no AOSP reference to copy: AOSP's own
         frameworks/base/core/res/res/xml/power_profile.xml does not contain
         bluetooth.controller.* at all, and the only file in the platform that
         does is frameworks/base/tools/powermodel/test-resource/
         power_profile.xml:157-166, a unit-test fixture. Measure them (idle, rx
         and tx controller current in mA, plus voltage in mV) before adding
         them; do not paste a fixture's numbers. Cost of the gap is
         misattributed battery blame only. -->
    <item name="bluetooth.active">60</item>
    <item name="bluetooth.on">3.24</item>
    <item name="wifi.on">4.7</item>
    <item name="wifi.active">60</item>
    <item name="wifi.scan">25</item>
    <!-- Android 10 renamed the donor's dsp.audio/dsp.video keys. -->
    <item name="audio">74</item>
    <item name="video">132</item>
    <item name="camera.flashlight">80</item>
    <!-- UNMEASURED PLACEHOLDER, not a measurement of this handset.

         camera.avg was 0, and 0 here means the two cameras are blamed for
         nothing at all: CameraPowerCalculator.java:29 reads this key and :43
         computes cameraPowerMah as cameraTimeMs * value / 3600000, so a zero
         makes every camera session free in Settings, Battery.

         550 is AOSP's own example real-world value for this key, from the
         comment on frameworks/base/core/res/res/xml/power_profile.xml:41;
         that file states at :21-29 that its literal values are deliberately
         incorrect dummies and that the example values live in the comments.
         AOSP's power_profile_test.xml:94 uses 600, so the order of magnitude
         is agreed.

         COST IF WRONG: misattributed battery blame in the Settings battery
         breakdown and in BatteryStats dumps. Nothing functional; this file is
         accounting metadata only.

         TO MEASURE: at a fixed brightness and airplane mode, read
         /sys/class/power_supply/battery/current_now with the screen on and
         idle, then again with a camera preview running, and take the
         difference in mA. Do that on both camera ids and keep the larger. -->
    <item name="camera.avg">550</item>
    <item name="gps.on">3</item>
    <item name="radio.active">82</item>
    <item name="radio.scanning">32</item>
    <array name="radio.on">
        <value>5</value>
        <value>4.5</value>
        <value>4</value>
        <value>3.5</value>
        <value>3</value>
    </array>

    <array name="cpu.clusters.cores">
        <value>4</value>
        <value>4</value>
    </array>

    <!-- cpu0-3: measured OPPs; currents interpolated from the Vivo curve. -->
    <array name="cpu.core_speeds.cluster0">
        <value>1001000</value>
        <value>910000</value>
        <value>819000</value>
        <value>689000</value>
        <value>598000</value>
        <value>494000</value>
        <value>338000</value>
        <value>156000</value>
    </array>
    <array name="cpu.core_power.cluster0">
        <value>108.1</value>
        <value>99.9</value>
        <value>82.5</value>
        <value>59.4</value>
        <value>48.9</value>
        <value>42.1</value>
        <value>35</value>
        <value>35</value>
    </array>

    <!-- cpu4-7: measured OPPs; currents interpolated from the Vivo curve. -->
    <array name="cpu.core_speeds.cluster1">
        <value>1508000</value>
        <value>1430000</value>
        <value>1352000</value>
        <value>1196000</value>
        <value>1027000</value>
        <value>871000</value>
        <value>663000</value>
        <value>286000</value>
    </array>
    <array name="cpu.core_power.cluster1">
        <value>177.1</value>
        <value>166.8</value>
        <value>155.2</value>
        <value>132.5</value>
        <value>111.4</value>
        <value>97.4</value>
        <value>56.9</value>
        <value>35</value>
    </array>

    <!-- Android's 2017 power-model migration maps old cpu.idle to suspend,
         old cpu.awake to idle, and old cpu.active[] to per-core power. The
         donor has no separate global/cluster overhead, so keep those at zero
         rather than double-counting its per-core curve. -->
    <item name="cpu.suspend">5</item>
    <item name="cpu.idle">35</item>
    <item name="cpu.active">0</item>
    <item name="cpu.cluster_power.cluster0">0</item>
    <item name="cpu.cluster_power.cluster1">0</item>

    <array name="memory.bandwidths">
        <value>0</value>
    </array>

    <!-- Nominal capacity from the cell's own printing: 3.8 V / 12.16 Wh = 3200 mAh
         (teardown, E-176). Keep this equal to the kernel's Q_MAX_POS_25 in
         mt_battery_meter.h, which is what the driver exports as charge_full;
         a disagreement makes BatteryStats' mAh and the gauge's percentage
         describe two different packs. -->
    <item name="battery.capacity">3200</item>

    <item name="wifi.controller.idle">0</item>
    <item name="wifi.controller.rx">0</item>
    <item name="wifi.controller.tx">0</item>
    <array name="wifi.controller.tx_levels" />
    <item name="wifi.controller.voltage">0</item>
    <array name="wifi.batchedscan">
        <value>0.0002</value>
        <value>0.002</value>
        <value>0.02</value>
        <value>0.2</value>
        <value>2</value>
    </array>

    <!-- Cellular IS attributed, through the legacy radio.* path above. But
         NOT for the reason the value zero suggests, and the previous version of
         this comment had the rule backwards.

         MobileRadioPowerCalculator.java:52-77 selects on key PRESENCE, not on
         value: getAveragePowerOrDefault(POWER_RADIO_ACTIVE, -1) and
         (POWER_RADIO_ON, -1) short-circuit the per-controller branch whenever
         radio.active and radio.on exist at all, whatever these five contain.
         So modem.controller.idle / rx / tx below are UNREACHABLE rather than
         "zero and therefore inert", and measuring them later changes nothing
         unless radio.active and radio.on are DELETED first.

         modem.controller.sleep and .voltage are not read by that calculator at
         all; their only consumer is BatteryStatsImpl.java:11052, the raw
         ModemActivityInfo energy path. Two consumers, not one model.

         Said explicitly because five zeros immediately after a 23-line note
         about the harm of a zeroed component reads like the same bug. -->
    <item name="modem.controller.sleep">0</item>
    <item name="modem.controller.idle">0</item>
    <item name="modem.controller.rx">0</item>
    <array name="modem.controller.tx">
        <value>0</value>
        <value>0</value>
        <value>0</value>
        <value>0</value>
        <value>0</value>
    </array>
    <item name="modem.controller.voltage">0</item>

    <!-- GNSS blame is one flat donor constant: gps.on = 3 mA above, carried
         over rather than measured here.

         These two values are DEAD while that key exists.
         SensorPowerCalculator.getAverageGpsPower()
         (frameworks/base/core/java/com/android/internal/os/
         SensorPowerCalculator.java:68-74) selects on key PRESENCE:
         getAveragePowerOrDefault(POWER_GPS_ON, -1) returns immediately whenever
         gps.on is in the file, so the signal-quality loop below it never runs.
         (The previous version of this comment named GnssPowerCalculator, which
         does not exist in Q; that class arrives in Android 12. Its "TO MEASURE"
         instruction would therefore have produced nothing.)

         To USE signal-quality differentiation, gps.on has to be DELETED as well
         as this array filled. The run is the same controlled one the Bluetooth
         values need: one window with a fix held at strong signal, one at weak,
         screen off and modem idle. Until then the flat constant is what is
         actually in effect and these zeros are honest. -->
    <array name="gps.signalqualitybased">
        <value>0</value>
        <value>0</value>
    </array>
    <item name="gps.voltage">0</item>
</device>
